    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 1-3', r'''
# Known limitations

Things that were found, decided and left alone (upstream behaviour, by design, or not worth the risk). Each is verified by hand; none loses data. If one bites you, open an issue.'''),
    ...text('This is a register, not a bug tracker. Every item has a category in its bullet: documented not fixed, not fixed because low, not fixed because decided, known and not fixed, and the dedicated groups for the macros phase and the platforms. A reader who hits something can search the file for the symptom and find either a workaround or a statement that nobody can fix it from here.'),
    ...sec('documented, not fixed'),
    ...text('The first group is the long list. Three kinds of entry mix in it. Upstream behaviours that cannot be changed from here: the multi-location breakpoint on a format! line, where lldb resolves three locations and continue stops three times; gr waiting 300 ms because Neovim 0.12 makes it a prefix of grr, gra and friends. Language server behaviours: moving a module file out of the module tree with leader cR leaves the mod declaration. And limits of this config’s own design: files inside a submodule are not in the parent’s Local History, only the gitlink, while the submodule’s own repository gets them.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 5-5', r'''
- **Documented, not fixed** (upstream, by design, or not worth the risk): the multi-location breakpoint on a `format!` line (lldb resolves 3 locations, `continue` stops 3 times; there are no F-key maps); an LSP multi-file rename is undone per buffer (`u` in one file leaves the others renamed; auto-save then writes the half state); moving a module file out of the module tree with `<leader>cR` leaves the `mod` declaration (rust-analyzer); `<leader>rR`, `rP`, `<A-j>`, `<A-k>` give no feedback; the inc-rename preview omits occurrences inside macro arguments (the commit changes them); `gr` waits 300 ms because nvim 0.12's `grr`/`gra`/`grn`/`gri`/`grt`/`grx` make it a prefix; `<leader>dr` before rust-analyzer is ready does nothing; a panic stops in `__rustc::rust_panic` with the workspace frame six up; a compile error during a neotest run keeps the old results (see `<leader>to`); flamegraph leaves `flamegraph.svg` and `perf.data` in the project root; an edit inside a submodule is not in the parent's local history (only the gitlink; the submodule's own repo gets it on its `refs/lochist`); the persisted `:RustContext` choice has no statusline indicator; crates.nvim reports "upgrade available" twice and taplo hovers next to crates.nvim in `Cargo.toml`; mini.pairs after an escaped quote leaves a stray `"`; the coverage summary float may have a doubled border; toggle notifications show `**bold**` asterisks; `99.log` is truncated per nvim session (upstream); any other lint that the two tiers word and code differently still shows twice (only `unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn` / `E0133` is mapped); a line with many diagnostics shows a run of bullets without a count; the persisted `:RustContext` choice has no indicator; `~/.claude/jobs`, `state`, `backups` and `daemon.*` stay writable inside the 99 sandbox (making them read-only was not tried against a real claude); `treesj` has no `match_block`, plain-string `Regex::new("...")` gets no regex injection, doc comments get no markdown injection; wgsl-analyzer's "Project discovery failed" error message on every `.wgsl` open; the codelldb REPL is in commands mode (`?expr` evaluates, `sum + 100` does not); kulala's first scripted request waits for its `npm install` and rollup build (about 70 s, needs network); a standalone `.rs` opened after a crate file gets no diagnostics; an unwritable `XDG_CACHE_HOME` or `XDG_STATE_HOME` gives an init.lua / lazy.nvim E739; `<leader>rm` on built-in derives says "No macro under cursor!" (use `cargo expand`).'''),
    ...text('The same line contains one sandbox limit: the ~/.claude jobs, state, backups and daemon files stay writable, and making them read-only was not tried against a real claude. One item appears twice in the line, the persisted RustContext choice having no statusline indicator, which is a harmless duplication in the source.'),
    ...sec('not fixed, low and not fixed, decided'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 7-7', r'''
- Not fixed (low, as in the field test list): spell noise on Rust words, `rl` kind labels, snippets mid-expression, kulala `Rq`, bigfile undo files, `.http` conceal, XDG dirs unwritable, picker `target/` without `.gitignore`, dashboard title, the README items of the field test's docs-only list that round 1 already changed.'''),
    ...text('The two shorter bullets separate cosmetic things (spell noise on Rust words, a conceal level on .http files, the dashboard title) from items where a decision was made after looking, such as the neotest no test at cursor notice that could not be reproduced, extract assists without a rename prompt, and a DAP attach configuration. One decided item is a build fact: bootstrap.sh exits before Mason finishes its async downloads and Mason resumes on the next start.'),
    ...sec('known and not fixed'),
    ...text('This group has the items most likely to surprise a user. Two Neovim instances on the same workspace share bacon-ls’s single shadow directory, so one’s unsaved text can show up as clippy errors in the other; the advice is one nvim per workspace. A multi-file rename is undone per buffer, and the revert is Local History. Typing hitches in a 1 MB, 50k-line Rust file, cause not found. A stored RustContext feature that no longer exists makes clippy fail, and the cure is :RustContext reset. neotest shows ignored tests and doctests as passed after an all-green file run. The last sentence tells people on an existing install to run :Lazy clean once for the 18 dropped theme plugins.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 11-11', r'''
- **Known and not fixed** (decided): two nvims on the same workspace share bacon-ls's single `target/bacon-ls-live/shadow`, so one's unsaved text can show up as clippy errors in the other (upstream; open one nvim per workspace); a multi-file rename is undone per buffer (`u` in one file leaves the others renamed: use `<leader>gH` local history to revert it); typing hitches in a 1 MB / 50k-line Rust file (cause not found); clippy waits while another cargo holds `~/.cargo`'s package-cache lock; a stored `:RustContext` feature that no longer exists makes clippy fail (`:RustContext reset`); `:RustRestart` leaves clippy blind to unsaved text until the next edit; after editing a proc-macro crate use `:RustRestart` (`reloadWorkspace` does not clear "proc-macro map is missing"); neotest shows `#[ignore]` tests and doctests as passed after an all-green file run (use `<leader>tD`); `<CR>` accepts blink's preselected fuzzy match; the statusline drops the file name at 80 columns; Trouble and raw LSP messages of bacon-ls items keep the full multi-line rustc text; run `:Lazy clean` once on an existing install for the 18 dropped theme plugins.'''),
    ...sec('the macros and dependencies field test'),
    ...text('The next bullet scopes a different set of findings: things found while testing the macros phase that the phase did not cause. A slow leader r key in a non-Rust buffer, because the hidden stubs give which-key nothing to hold. A comment or string accepting blink’s preselect. Local History with a corrupt private index, long branch names or info/exclude. :RustNew in a directory with a hash or percent sign. bacon-ls with a space in the project path. A pinned toolchain without rust-analyzer. And two limits that belong to the phase itself: a dependency glob such as use serde::* silences the unknown-crate hint for the whole file, and buffers over 20000 lines get no hint and no syntax-scan auto-import.'),
    ...text('The decision to list them here instead of fixing them follows the README rule: the phase is documented with its limits, and a failure outside the phase is tracked where it was found.'),
    ...sec('other 99 providers are not sandboxed'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 14-14', r'''
- **Other 99 providers are not sandboxed**: `<leader>9P` can switch to OpenCode, Cursor, Gemini or Kiro only under an explicit opt-in (`vim.g.xynovim_99_sandbox = false`, or `vim.g.xynovim_99_allow_unsandboxed = true` when no sandbox backend exists). They run with no sandbox, no snapshot and no auto-save pause on any OS; the opt-in is re-checked on every request (revoked: the request is refused) and each request warns. Only the Claude Code provider is guarded.'''),
    ...text('This one is a deliberate design statement. The provider picker can switch to OpenCode, Cursor, Gemini or Kiro only under an explicit opt-in, and then those providers run with no sandbox, no snapshot and no auto-save pause on any OS. The opt-in is re-checked on every request and revoked means refused, and every request warns. Only the Claude Code provider is guarded. The history behind it is the unreleased CHANGELOG entry about the provider picker.'),
    ...sec('Windows and macOS: what the workflow shows'),
    ...text('The final bullet is the longest and the most careful. It starts by limiting the evidence to four things: that nvim starts headlessly without stderr output (which says nothing about the Rust or LSP path), the lockfile restore, the bootstrap script as checked by workflow steps only, and the spec files that are not skipped. Then it lists which spec files are skipped whole on Windows by an xyno-os header, with the reason for each. It insists that most of those skips are tooling limits (specs use sh stubs, /proc, kill -0 or sed -i), not features known to be Linux-only.'),
    ...code('markdown', r'docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md · lines 15-15', r'''
- **Windows and macOS: what the `platforms` workflow shows (runs 39647013043, 39655339161, 39660597368)**. Evidence is limited to: `nvim --headless +qa` starts without stderr output (no time bound, and it never loads the Rust or LSP path, so it shows that plugins restore and nvim starts, not that the Rust features start), the lockfile restore, the bootstrap script (checked by the workflow steps only, there is no spec for `bootstrap.ps1`), and the spec files that are not skipped. Run 39660597368: the Windows job passed, but 11 of the 38 spec files are skipped whole there by `-- xyno-os:` headers (0 passed, 1 skipped each), so nothing in them is demonstrated on Windows: `agent-99-guard` (it was skipped by a path-separator bug in the spec's own guard, not a feature limit; fixed, and run 39668277143 then ran it on Windows: 50 passed, 3 failed on path-form assertions of the spec, fixed in the next commit), `agent-sandbox` (bubblewrap, Linux only by design), `bacon-ls-config`, `egui-new`, `health-bootstrap-ci`, `local-history` (the headline safety net: no Windows evidence at all), `rust-analyzer-config`, `rust-new`, `server-supervision-statusline`, `startup-and-options` (so no spec covers startup options on Windows or macOS) and `test-infra-readme-claims`. Most of those skip because the specs use sh/bash stubs, `/proc`, `kill -0` or `sed -i`, not because the feature is known to be Linux-only; they are tooling-limited skips, not features shown to work. On macOS `startup-and-options`, `server-supervision-statusline`, `rust-analyzer-config`, `agent-sandbox` and `test-infra-readme-claims` skip the same way. The 99 agent refuses to start on both (no sandbox backend, `agent-failclosed` spec).'''),
    ...text('The second paragraph of the bullet lists failures without a diagnosed cause and says how they are marked: skipped in the specs with a printed reason through H.known_issue, searchable as known issue on. On Windows, bacon-ls does not attach to a fresh crate; the diagnostic printed an empty exepath for bacon-ls and bacon, and the Mason log shows bacon installation aborted, so the likely cause is PATH, not an LSP fault, and the text says explicitly that the cause is not confirmed. Live rust-analyzer flows fail or hang on Windows. codelldb does not install through Mason on either OS. On macOS several timing specs are flaky, and run 39673715062 failed in the plugin restore because a treesitter parser would not compile.'),
    ...text('The final sentence explains why the platform jobs are not hidden: they stay in CI so that the skips are visible, and a green run is the evidence to cite before the README says anything about Windows or macOS. That is exactly how the README’s Platforms table is written.'),
    ...sec('design decisions in the file'),
